# libical -- the iCalendar implementation, and it is here for exactly one of the
# six libraries it ships: libical-glib.
#
# evolution-data-server 3.56.2 does not ask for libical. CMakeLists.txt:938 is
#
#     pkg_check_modules(GLIB REQUIRED ... libical-glib>=3.0.7 ...)
#
# so what it resolves, by name, through pkg-config, is libical-glib.pc. A
# libical built without the GObject binding installs libical.pc, libical.so,
# every header and a complete-looking package, and e-d-s stops at configure
# naming a module this recipe never produced. That is why the binding is a
# build option on THIS recipe rather than a second package, and why install.sh
# asserts libical-glib.pc rather than libical.pc: the assertion's correct value
# is a fact about the CONSUMER, not about the package.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env, which
# tools/pin-versions.sh regenerates wholesale from a fixed PACKAGES array.
#
# BLFS 12.4 carries 3.0.20. The tarball fetched from the URL below has md5
# 539a8a293d344e7aa8ccf3740494a46d, which is the md5 the book's page states.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/libical/libical/releases/download/v3.0.20/libical-3.0.20.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=e73de92f5a6ce84c1b00306446b290a2b08cdf0a80988eca0a2c9d5c3510b4c2
SRC_DIR=libical-3.0.20

# ===========================================================================
# THE ONE THAT IS SILENT: -DUSE_BUILTIN_TZDATA=True
# ===========================================================================
#
# Its default is FALSE, meaning "use the system timezone data on non-Windows
# systems". THIS TREE HAS NO SYSTEM TIMEZONE DATA, and nothing about that is
# visible from a build.
#
# src/libical/icaltz-util.c:104-109 is a fixed list of four directories:
#
#     /usr/share/zoneinfo  /usr/lib/zoneinfo  /etc/zoneinfo  /usr/share/lib/zoneinfo
#
# set_zonedir() takes the first one holding a zone.tab and leaves the directory
# NULL if none does. Measured in a container seeded from the published index
# rather than inferred: all four are absent, and there is no zone.tab anywhere
# on the filesystem. tzdata is an LFS chapter 8 package (8.5, installed by hand
# inside the Glibc page) and no recipe here ships it.
#
# What that costs, and why no check would find it. icaltimezone.c:1685-1700
# takes the !use_builtin_tzdata branch, gets a NULL directory, computes
# filename_len == 0, sets ICAL_INTERNAL_ERROR and RETURNS -- with the
# builtin_timezones array left empty. icalerror_assert does not abort, because
# ICAL_ERRORS_ARE_FATAL defaults to 0 (CMakeLists.txt:317-327). So the package
# compiles, installs, ships every library and every header, satisfies
# libical-glib.pc, satisfies e-d-s's configure, and answers every
# icaltimezone_get_builtin_timezone() call with NULL. An e-d-s on top of it
# files every appointment against no timezone at all.
#
# -DUSE_BUILTIN_TZDATA=True adds the zoneinfo subdirectory to the build
# (CMakeLists.txt:693-696 -- the only thing that installs it) and flips the
# initial value of the static use_builtin_tzdata (icaltimezone.c:130-134), so
# the library reads /usr/share/libical/zoneinfo, which it now ships. BLFS
# documents this exact switch under Command Explanations; BLFS systems simply
# never need it, because the book's own chapter 8 put tzdata there.
#
# THE ASSERTION IS THE INSTALLED DATA, NOT A STRING IN THE BINARY. Grepping
# libical.so for "/usr/share/libical/zoneinfo" would pass a WRONG build:
# get_zone_directory_builtin() is compiled unconditionally, so ZONEINFO_DIRECTORY
# is in .rodata whichever way the flag went. The zoneinfo tree, by contrast, is
# installed only by the branch the flag controls. See install.sh.
#
# If a tzdata package ever lands here, this is the decision to re-open -- the
# builtin data is a copy libical does not promise to keep current. Stated as a
# fact to re-check rather than as a claim about what that package would do.
#
# ===========================================================================
# THE ONE THAT IS LOUD: -DICAL_BUILD_DOCS=False
# ===========================================================================
#
# Not tidiness, and not a build-time saving -- with the upstream defaults this
# package does not configure in this tree at all. ICAL_BUILD_DOCS defaults True
# (CMakeLists.txt:421), doc/CMakeLists.txt adds doc/reference, which adds
# doc/reference/libical-glib whenever ICAL_GLIB is on, whose own ENABLE_GTK_DOC
# defaults True -- and that file includes GtkDoc.cmake, line 28 of which is
#
#     message(FATAL_ERROR "Cannot find all gtk-doc binaries, install them or
#                          use -DENABLE_GTK_DOC=OFF instead")
#
# gtk-doc is not packaged here. Recorded because the failure direction is the
# good one and worth knowing when reading this file: this flag cannot be lost
# silently, unlike every other one above and below it.
#
# ENABLE_GTK_DOC IS DELIBERATELY NOT PASSED. With docs off, doc/ is never added,
# so the option is never declared and passing it would only produce a
# "Manually-specified variables were not used" warning -- a flag whose sole
# effect is to describe a subdirectory that does not exist in this build. The
# outcome is pinned by ICAL_BUILD_DOCS; this is the one place where stating the
# same thing twice would be worth less than stating it once.
#
# ===========================================================================
# THE ONE THE BOOK GETS WRONG FOR US: -DICAL_GLIB_VAPI=False
# ===========================================================================
#
# BLFS 12.4's command line passes -D ICAL_GLIB_VAPI=true. Copying it verbatim
# fails at configure here: CMakeLists.txt:449-461 does find_program(VALAC) and
# find_program(VAPIGEN) and raises FATAL_ERROR on either being absent, and Vala
# is not packaged in this tree. The book's line is correct for a system that
# followed the book, which is the assumption a BLFS command line is allowed to
# make and a recipe here is not.
#
# False is also the upstream default; it is passed explicitly because this
# comment names it, and a recipe that gets its behaviour by saying nothing is
# one whose comments and flags drift apart with neither being wrong.
#
# ===========================================================================
# The GObject binding, which is the whole reason for the package
# ===========================================================================
#
# -DICAL_GLIB=True. Already the default (CMakeLists.txt:466), passed explicitly
# for the reason above -- and stated here because its dependency list is longer
# than the book's page suggests. It hard-requires BOTH glib >= 2.32 AND
# libxml >= 2.7.3, each a FATAL_ERROR when missing (CMakeLists.txt:492-510).
# BLFS lists libxml2 only under "Recommended"; the build file makes it required
# the moment the binding is on, which for us is always. libxml2 is used by the
# code GENERATOR rather than by the library -- see TAPEBUILD.toml, where that
# distinction decides which section it goes in and does not come out where a
# reading of this file would put it.
#
# -DGOBJECT_INTROSPECTION=True. THE DEFAULT IS FALSE: CMakeLists.txt:383 is a
# bare option() with no value. Without this there is no ICalGLib-3.0.gir and no
# ICalGLib-3.0.typelib -- and e-d-s's own libecal introspection includes
# ICalGLib-3.0, which g-ir-scanner reads off disk. A missing .gir is not a link
# error and not a pkg-config failure; it is a scanner that cannot resolve an
# include, one package later. Same class as p11-kit's asn1Parser: an exec-time
# build dependency no linkage check can see.
#
# -DWITH_CXX_BINDINGS=True, the upstream default, passed explicitly. Nothing in
# this tree consumes libical_cxx or libicalss_cxx today; they are kept because
# BLFS ships them and removing them would be a divergence bought with nothing.
# The cost is one libstdc++ link, which gcc already supplies to every package.
#
# ===========================================================================
# The rest
# ===========================================================================
#
# -DSHARED_ONLY=True. BLFS passes it. It drops the static archives and, with
# them, the ical-glib-static target (src/libical-glib/CMakeLists.txt:128-133),
# which exists only in the both-libraries case.
#
# -DLIBICAL_BUILD_TESTING=False. Defaults True. It installs nothing, and it
# pulls in src/test/libical-glib, whose CMakeLists.txt:1 is
# find_program(PYTHON3 python3) -- an undeclared build tool that happens to be
# present in the seed.
#
# -DLIBICAL_BUILD_EXAMPLES=False, and this one is a lie in the tarball's own
# header. Lines 69-72 document it as an option defaulting to true. THERE IS NO
# option() FOR IT ANYWHERE IN THE TREE: the only uses are that comment, the
# ReleaseNotes entry announcing it, one explicit -D in scripts/buildtests.sh,
# and CMakeLists.txt:690, which tests an undefined variable and is therefore
# always false. So examples never build unless someone passes the flag, the
# documented default is wrong, and passing False changes nothing today. Passed
# anyway, so that an upstream release which finally declares the option -- with
# the default its own header has claimed for years -- cannot start building
# examples in a package whose description names none.
#
# NOT PASSED, deliberately:
#
#   ICU                 There is no option. find_package(ICU) at
#                       CMakeLists.txt:214 is unconditional and unguarded, and
#                       icu is published, so CI seeds it and RSCALE support
#                       (RFC 7529, non-Gregorian recurrence) switches ITSELF on
#                       -- along with "Requires.private: icu-i18n" in
#                       libical.pc. That is an autodetected dependency decided
#                       by what is present at build time, so it is declared in
#                       TAPEBUILD.toml and ASSERTED in install.sh rather than
#                       left to be discovered by whoever builds in an image
#                       without it. There is a fatal edge behind it worth
#                       knowing: CMakeLists.txt:232-245 needs one of five ICU
#                       PROGRAMS to locate the runtime path and raises
#                       FATAL_ERROR ("Is your ICU installation broken?") when it
#                       finds libraries and no program. This tree's icu recipe
#                       asserts /usr/bin/icuinfo, which is one of the five.
#   BerkeleyDB          find_package(BerkeleyDB), TYPE OPTIONAL, not packaged
#                       here, so HAVE_BDB stays unset and libicalss builds
#                       without its Berkeley DB store. A property of the image
#                       rather than of this recipe, which is why it is stated
#                       rather than pinned.
#   ICAL_ERRORS_ARE_FATAL   left at its default of 0. Turning it on would make
#                       the tzdata failure above abort instead of returning
#                       quietly -- which sounds attractive and is not: it
#                       changes the abort behaviour of every icalerror_* call
#                       in every consumer, and the failure it would have caught
#                       is now prevented rather than detected.
#   BUILD_SHARED_LIBS   _scripts/build-cmake.sh passes ON already.
CMAKE_ARGS="-DSHARED_ONLY=True \
-DICAL_GLIB=True -DGOBJECT_INTROSPECTION=True -DICAL_GLIB_VAPI=False \
-DWITH_CXX_BINDINGS=True \
-DUSE_BUILTIN_TZDATA=True \
-DICAL_BUILD_DOCS=False \
-DLIBICAL_BUILD_TESTING=False -DLIBICAL_BUILD_EXAMPLES=False"
